topics.description:
**Custom properties** (CSS variables) store values you reuse across a stylesheet. Unlike Sass variables they live in the browser: they **cascade**, **inherit** and can be changed at runtime.

### Declare and use
```css
:root {
  --main-color: #0062ff;
  --bg-color: #080a0c;
  --radius: 8px;
}
.btn {
  background: var(--main-color);
  border-radius: var(--radius);
}
```

- Names start with `--` and are case-sensitive.
- `:root` (the html element) is the usual place for global tokens.
- Use `var(--name, fallback)` to provide a default.

### They cascade and inherit
Override a variable inside any selector and only that subtree changes:

```css
.card.danger { --main-color: crimson; }
```

### Why use them
- Single source of truth for a theme (colors, spacing, fonts).
- Change one value and the whole site updates (the final project in the course uses `--main-color`, `--bg-color` etc.).
- Runtime theming (dark mode) and JavaScript control: `element.style.setProperty('--x', value)`.

### Not just colors
Any value: lengths, numbers, strings, even partial values like `--shadow: 0 4px 12px rgba(0,0,0,.2);`.

### Remember
- Variables cannot be used in media query conditions.
- If a variable is undefined and no fallback exists, the property becomes invalid at computed time.

examples.css:
:root { --main-color: #0062ff; --font-color: #fff; --radius: 8px; --pad: 10px 20px; }
.btn { background: var(--main-color); color: var(--font-color); border: 2px solid var(--main-color); border-radius: var(--radius); padding: var(--pad); font: bold 15px sans-serif; }
.danger { --main-color: #e11d48; }
.ghost { background: transparent; color: var(--main-color); }

examples.js:
const box = document.querySelector('.box');
let hue = 220;
document.getElementById('b').addEventListener('click', () => {
  hue = (hue + 50) % 360;
  box.style.setProperty('--hue', hue);
});

topics.description:
Beyond simple colors, custom properties enable **design systems** and powerful tricks.

### 1. Design tokens in layers
```css
:root {
  /* primitives */
  --blue-600: #2563eb; --space-2: .5rem;
  /* semantic tokens */
  --color-primary: var(--blue-600);
  --color-surface: #fff;
}
[data-theme="dark"] { --color-surface: #0f172a; }
```

### 2. Component API (configurable components)
```css
.btn { --btn-bg: var(--color-primary); --btn-pad: .5rem 1rem;
  background: var(--btn-bg); padding: var(--btn-pad); }
.btn.large { --btn-pad: 1rem 2rem; }
```

### 3. Math with variables
```css
.grid { --cols: 4; grid-template-columns: repeat(var(--cols), 1fr); }
.item { animation-delay: calc(var(--i) * 80ms); }
```
Use `calc(var(--n) * 1px)` - a unitless variable can be given any unit.

### 4. Registered properties with @property
Gives a variable a **type**, initial value and makes it animatable:

```css
@property --angle { syntax: "<angle>"; inherits: false; initial-value: 0deg; }
.spin { background: conic-gradient(from var(--angle), red, blue, red); animation: turn 3s linear infinite; }
@keyframes turn { to { --angle: 360deg; } }
```

### 5. The "space toggle" and fallbacks
Nested fallbacks: `var(--a, var(--b, red))`.

### 6. Scoped theming
Set tokens on any wrapper to restyle everything below it - perfect for cards, sections and previews.

### Tips
- Name by purpose (`--color-danger`), not value (`--red`).
- Keep tokens in one file.
- Beware of too many layers: prefer clarity.

examples.css:
.theme-a { --btn-bg: #2563eb; } .theme-b { --btn-bg: #16a34a; }
div { padding: 10px; }
.btn { --btn-pad: .5rem 1rem; background: var(--btn-bg, #333); color: #fff; padding: var(--btn-pad); border: 0; border-radius: 8px; font: bold 14px sans-serif; }
.btn.large { --btn-pad: 1rem 2rem; font-size: 18px; }

@property --angle { syntax: "<angle>"; inherits: false; initial-value: 0deg; }
.ring { width: 120px; height: 120px; border-radius: 50%; background: conic-gradient(from var(--angle), #f06, #48f, #0fc, #f06); animation: turn 3s linear infinite; }
@keyframes turn { to { --angle: 360deg; } }

topics.description:
**Cascade layers** let you declare the *priority order of groups of styles*. A layer that comes later beats an earlier one **regardless of specificity**.

### Syntax
```css
/* 1. declare the order once */
@layer reset, base, components, utilities;

/* 2. fill layers anywhere */
@layer reset {
  * { margin: 0; box-sizing: border-box; }
}
@layer components {
  .btn { background: royalblue; padding: .5rem 1rem; }
}
@layer utilities {
  .bg-red { background: crimson; }   /* wins over .btn even with lower specificity */
}
```

### Important rules
- Order is set by the **first** declaration of layer names.
- **Unlayered** styles beat all layered styles (they are treated as the final layer).
- For `!important` the order **reverses**: earlier layers win.
- Layers can be nested: `@layer framework.theme { }`.
- Import a whole library into a layer: `@import url(lib.css) layer(vendor);`

### Why it matters
- Third-party CSS (Bootstrap, plugins) goes into a low layer, so your styles always win without `!important`.
- Resets and base styles can never accidentally beat components.
- Utilities in the top layer override components predictably.

### Tools in the same family
`:where()` (zero specificity) and `@scope` help with the same problem from other angles.

### Debugging
DevTools shows the layer a rule belongs to. If a style does not apply, check for an unlayered rule overriding it.

examples.css:
@layer base, components, utilities;
@layer components {
  #save.btn { background: royalblue; color: #fff; padding: 10px 20px; border: 0; font: bold 15px sans-serif; border-radius: 6px; }  /* high specificity */
}
@layer utilities {
  .btn { background: crimson; }   /* low specificity, but later layer wins */
}

